WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.285
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.188 --> 00:00:05.481
Hi, hello everyone,
my name is Colin.

00:00:05.482 --> 00:00:09.311
I have the foolish ambition
of trying to get through two

00:00:09.312 --> 00:00:12.510
projects in ten minutes
right after lunch.

00:00:12.511 --> 00:00:14.493
So hopefully,
this will wake you all up.

00:00:14.494 --> 00:00:19.350
The first project is what
we call Project Karya.

00:00:19.351 --> 00:00:20.656
Karya means work in Hindi.

00:00:20.657 --> 00:00:25.540
And the goal of Project Karya
is to provide dignified digital

00:00:25.541 --> 00:00:26.216
labor.

00:00:27.665 --> 00:00:30.925
I think most of you in
the audience probably know about

00:00:30.926 --> 00:00:33.490
this notion of crowdsourcing.

00:00:33.491 --> 00:00:36.603
So the basic idea behind
crowdsourcing is that we're

00:00:36.604 --> 00:00:39.030
going to take a large
scale digital task.

00:00:39.031 --> 00:00:41.120
Preferably digital
tasks that are hard for

00:00:41.121 --> 00:00:43.230
our computers to automate.

00:00:43.231 --> 00:00:46.300
And we're going to chop these
tasks into millions of little

00:00:46.301 --> 00:00:49.280
pieces, what we
called microwork.

00:00:49.281 --> 00:00:53.335
Then we're going to give this
microwork to humans, to people,

00:00:53.336 --> 00:00:54.394
to accomplish.

00:00:54.395 --> 00:00:57.014
And then we're going to collect
the results at the end,

00:00:57.015 --> 00:00:58.197
that's the basic idea.

00:00:58.198 --> 00:01:02.364
Now what's unique about Project
Karya is the demographic of

00:01:02.365 --> 00:01:05.480
workers that we're targeting.

00:01:05.481 --> 00:01:09.360
So the typical crowdsourcing
worker today is college

00:01:09.361 --> 00:01:13.510
educated, feels relatively
comfortable with spoken and

00:01:13.511 --> 00:01:17.820
written English, and
often has access to a laptop or

00:01:17.821 --> 00:01:19.450
a desktop computer.

00:01:19.451 --> 00:01:22.760
Now the population that we're
targeting here are rural

00:01:22.761 --> 00:01:23.930
Indians.

00:01:23.931 --> 00:01:26.108
Typically low income, people who
may not have any of those three

00:01:26.109 --> 00:01:27.168
traits that I've mentioned.

00:01:29.578 --> 00:01:33.010
So, our goal with Project Karya
is to provide employment

00:01:33.011 --> 00:01:35.090
opportunities for these people.

00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:38.660
And as much as that,
we're also trying to

00:01:38.661 --> 00:01:43.080
help them build a set of digital
skills, IT skills for example.

00:01:43.081 --> 00:01:45.840
So that they can build a resume
over time and hopefully get

00:01:45.841 --> 00:01:48.960
a job of their own once
they're done with our project.

00:01:48.961 --> 00:01:49.820
And all the while of course,

00:01:49.821 --> 00:01:52.620
we're trying to accomplish
useful tasks and

00:01:52.621 --> 00:01:54.280
collect useful data.

00:01:54.281 --> 00:01:58.390
So you can think of Project
Karya as much as a social

00:01:58.391 --> 00:02:02.390
welfare program, as it is
a crowdsourcing platform.

00:02:02.391 --> 00:02:03.410
In fact in the long term,

00:02:03.411 --> 00:02:06.050
we're hoping to partner
with the Indian government

00:02:06.051 --> 00:02:09.480
to help scale this up as
a social welfare program.

00:02:09.481 --> 00:02:13.650
The technology we have,
here we have a server

00:02:13.651 --> 00:02:17.740
that takes in some task, like
a pile of handwritten forms.

00:02:17.741 --> 00:02:20.870
And then chops that task into
individual pieces of microwork,

00:02:20.871 --> 00:02:23.302
like pictures of individual
words in that form.

00:02:23.303 --> 00:02:26.384
And then hands it out to
an Android application

00:02:26.385 --> 00:02:29.624
that workers can use to
accomplish those tasks,

00:02:29.625 --> 00:02:31.849
gain skills, earn money, etc.

00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:33.833
And then we collect all of
the results at the end.

00:02:35.039 --> 00:02:37.450
The range of task that we're
targeting is fairly broad.

00:02:37.451 --> 00:02:39.930
We're looking at
image classification,

00:02:39.931 --> 00:02:44.060
formed digitization translation,
even tasks like GeoTagging.

00:02:44.061 --> 00:02:46.583
So going out with your phone and
taking pictures of things or

00:02:46.584 --> 00:02:48.098
even taking sensor measurements,

00:02:48.099 --> 00:02:50.177
maybe taking soil
measurements in rural areas.

00:02:50.178 --> 00:02:53.522
We've been working in
earnest on this project for

00:02:53.523 --> 00:02:54.802
about six months.

00:02:54.803 --> 00:02:57.330
There is a beautiful photo
of rural Maharashtra

00:02:57.331 --> 00:03:00.560
that Manu Jopra took, Manu is
kind of leading this project.

00:03:01.850 --> 00:03:04.464
So far we've done initial user
study in partnership with

00:03:04.465 --> 00:03:05.067
IAT Bombay.

00:03:05.068 --> 00:03:08.983
And we're dealing with
a Adivasi community in rural

00:03:08.984 --> 00:03:11.390
Maharashtra like I mentioned.

00:03:11.391 --> 00:03:15.698
Most of the users here actually
they have never actually used

00:03:15.699 --> 00:03:17.570
a smart phone before.

00:03:17.571 --> 00:03:20.617
So the task that we gave
them was actually fairly

00:03:20.618 --> 00:03:24.424
straightforward, which is on
the smartphone use a Marathi

00:03:24.425 --> 00:03:26.416
keyboard to enter their name.

00:03:26.417 --> 00:03:28.500
And the results were
pretty promising.

00:03:28.501 --> 00:03:30.294
There's a nice picture,

00:03:30.295 --> 00:03:33.319
you can see our first
user of Project Karya.

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:36.307
So there's a lot of things that
we've learned in the last six

00:03:36.308 --> 00:03:38.885
months, both in talking to
government agencies and

00:03:38.886 --> 00:03:40.245
doing these user studies.

00:03:40.246 --> 00:03:42.694
I'm just gonna focus on one
of the challenges that we've

00:03:42.695 --> 00:03:44.592
encountered which is
a very basic problem.

00:03:44.593 --> 00:03:47.017
These people do not
make very much money,

00:03:47.018 --> 00:03:49.665
they cannot afford very
much cellular data.

00:03:49.666 --> 00:03:53.331
So even if they're NGOs or
public service announcers or

00:03:53.332 --> 00:03:56.685
even for profit content
providers that want to give

00:03:56.686 --> 00:03:58.947
content for
free to these people,

00:03:58.948 --> 00:04:02.613
they can do so because the users
have to pay for the data,

00:04:02.614 --> 00:04:06.130
they can't afford data
download in the first place.

00:04:06.131 --> 00:04:08.526
So that brings me to
my next set of slides.

00:04:08.527 --> 00:04:12.571
Wait, the next project we
call people powered bulk

00:04:12.572 --> 00:04:14.280
data networking.

00:04:14.281 --> 00:04:17.449
There have been a lot of project
proposals in the past for

00:04:17.450 --> 00:04:19.950
how to get Internet to
the rest of the world.

00:04:19.951 --> 00:04:23.086
SpaceX in thinking about
low Earth orbit satellites,

00:04:23.087 --> 00:04:25.745
Facebook is thinking
about these big drones,

00:04:25.746 --> 00:04:28.214
Google is thinking
about these balloons.

00:04:28.215 --> 00:04:32.547
And what most of these projects
don't really understand is how

00:04:32.548 --> 00:04:36.715
people already make use of the
Internet today in a place rural

00:04:36.716 --> 00:04:37.298
Bihar.

00:04:37.299 --> 00:04:39.338
What these companies
like Google and

00:04:39.339 --> 00:04:42.496
Facebook are not realizing
is that, people have already

00:04:42.497 --> 00:04:45.456
figured out how to get their
hands on Internet content

00:04:45.457 --> 00:04:48.650
even if they can't afford
the Internet access.

00:04:48.651 --> 00:04:51.190
Namely, there
are these apps that

00:04:51.191 --> 00:04:53.890
people use to transfer
files between phones.

00:04:53.891 --> 00:04:56.020
So one of the phones
turns on a hotspot,

00:04:56.021 --> 00:04:57.220
the other phone connects
to the hotspot,

00:04:57.221 --> 00:05:00.390
you can transfer files at
very fast rates, okay?

00:05:00.391 --> 00:05:03.240
So this is really popular, this
is actually all over the world,

00:05:03.241 --> 00:05:05.210
not just India.

00:05:05.211 --> 00:05:07.340
The most popular of these
apps is called SHAREit, and

00:05:07.341 --> 00:05:09.740
they claim to have 200 million
users in India alone, so

00:05:09.741 --> 00:05:11.410
this is a massive thing.

00:05:11.411 --> 00:05:13.060
And before that,
before smartphones,

00:05:13.061 --> 00:05:14.040
there is of course Bluetooth,

00:05:14.041 --> 00:05:15.318
people have been doing this for
a long time.

00:05:15.319 --> 00:05:17.964
Now the way that this
is done today is,

00:05:17.965 --> 00:05:21.863
unfortunately still quite
limited, its quite ad hoc.

00:05:21.864 --> 00:05:24.969
And one of the biggest
limitations is that, I typically

00:05:24.970 --> 00:05:27.875
only share with people that
I know, so I have to go and

00:05:27.876 --> 00:05:30.912
talk to you and say, do you
have this movie that I want,

00:05:30.913 --> 00:05:33.680
I have this file,
should we share?

00:05:33.681 --> 00:05:36.950
I can't show it people like you
in this room where with in Wi-Fi

00:05:36.951 --> 00:05:38.960
range, we could be transferring
files right now but

00:05:38.961 --> 00:05:41.730
I don't have any content
discovering mechanism.

00:05:41.731 --> 00:05:43.970
So the basic research question
we're asking here is,

00:05:43.971 --> 00:05:47.650
if we provide some services
like content discovery,

00:05:47.651 --> 00:05:50.890
can we change people's behavior,
can we unable people to start

00:05:50.891 --> 00:05:54.710
sharing between people who
have never met each other?

00:05:54.711 --> 00:05:57.410
Let's start sharing in this
room because we have files that

00:05:57.411 --> 00:05:59.680
other people want.

00:05:59.681 --> 00:06:03.284
So what we have is pretty
straightforward that we just

00:06:03.285 --> 00:06:08.216
have some like cloud service,
That runs

00:06:08.217 --> 00:06:10.313
say in a South Indian
data center.

00:06:10.314 --> 00:06:14.231
And a content provider,
like I don't know some NGO,

00:06:14.232 --> 00:06:18.076
has some video they want
to distribute some people.

00:06:18.077 --> 00:06:19.769
They give us a copy
of that file and

00:06:19.770 --> 00:06:21.915
we extract some metadata
from that file.

00:06:21.916 --> 00:06:26.732
And then we run an Android app
on people's smartphones and

00:06:26.733 --> 00:06:31.353
this android app automatically
detects if I have some of

00:06:31.354 --> 00:06:35.696
the files that the content
providers have given us.

00:06:35.697 --> 00:06:38.259
Without actually having
to download all the data,

00:06:38.260 --> 00:06:41.800
it's just using metadata
like a hash of the content.

00:06:41.801 --> 00:06:44.860
And the other thing this app
does is it automatically detects

00:06:44.861 --> 00:06:47.660
if I'm within Wi-Fi
range about 80

00:06:47.661 --> 00:06:50.890
meters of someone else who has a
file that I want or vice versa,

00:06:50.891 --> 00:06:53.560
it will automatically
turn on the Wi-Fi and

00:06:53.561 --> 00:06:55.830
automatically perform
that transfer.

00:06:55.831 --> 00:06:59.442
Now one other cool feature is
that if the NGO or the content

00:06:59.443 --> 00:07:03.206
provider wants to incentivize
people to spread this kind of

00:07:03.207 --> 00:07:06.837
in a viral way, they might
even be willing to give money.

00:07:06.838 --> 00:07:08.671
I'm not gonna be able
to draw a rupee symbol,

00:07:08.672 --> 00:07:11.110
excuse me,
I'll draw a dollar sign instead.

00:07:11.111 --> 00:07:16.100
So if they want to incentivize
someone to spread this virally,

00:07:16.101 --> 00:07:18.860
the person who has the content
that's spreading it to the other

00:07:18.861 --> 00:07:21.500
person, they will
receive a mobile top up.

00:07:21.501 --> 00:07:23.580
This is like a form of
cellular data basically.

00:07:24.740 --> 00:07:28.150
So the idea here is that we can
make this spread virally by

00:07:28.151 --> 00:07:32.020
having them take the effort to
go walk to the next village and

00:07:32.021 --> 00:07:35.030
earn some mobile top ups by
giving it to other people, okay?

00:07:35.031 --> 00:07:36.620
Now from the users perspective,
this is great.

00:07:36.621 --> 00:07:39.020
I'm getting content show
up on my phone for free,

00:07:39.021 --> 00:07:40.880
I don't even have
to touch the phone.

00:07:40.881 --> 00:07:44.900
And even I might be able
to earn mobile top ups,

00:07:44.901 --> 00:07:46.660
just by simply running
an app on my phone.

00:07:46.661 --> 00:07:48.490
This is a really big
value proposition for

00:07:48.491 --> 00:07:50.010
people in rural India.

00:07:50.011 --> 00:07:51.710
And from the content
providers perspective,

00:07:51.711 --> 00:07:54.150
I think this is what's
perhaps most exciting,

00:07:54.151 --> 00:07:56.800
they have a way of reaching
a population which they didn't

00:07:56.801 --> 00:07:58.670
have a way to reach before.

00:07:58.671 --> 00:08:01.468
And another nice advantage is
that they can track how many

00:08:01.469 --> 00:08:03.158
times has this
file been watched,

00:08:03.159 --> 00:08:05.154
how many times has
it been transferred.

00:08:05.155 --> 00:08:07.591
And this enables opportunities
like advertising which is

00:08:07.592 --> 00:08:09.089
important for
someone like an NGO.

00:08:09.090 --> 00:08:11.915
And then the last opportunity
like I mentioned is,

00:08:11.916 --> 00:08:14.804
if they're willing to put in
a little bit of money for

00:08:14.805 --> 00:08:18.089
mobile top ups, they can cause
viral spread of some of these

00:08:18.090 --> 00:08:20.279
content that they
want to distribute.

00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:22.137
So that's actually
all I was gonna say.

00:08:22.138 --> 00:08:25.145
I'm really excited for
the panel discussion at the end.

00:08:25.146 --> 00:08:25.766
Thank you very much.

